          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"bronte-tenant-of-wildfell-hall-75ee1aa31adc6201\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":2,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"The embedded “I loved you” represents a loving state as a proposition to be evaluated in an imagined reversal of roles. It is not an actual avowal by Helen, so truth-conditional support is moderate rather than high. It neither undertakes a relational commitment nor functions as an affective verbal discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The clause has clear love-state content, but its embedding under “would you think” and a counterfactual condition weakens its assertoric force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"hypothetical\",\"description\":\"Helen asks Arthur to imagine their roles reversed and consider whether her analogous conduct would make him believe that she loved him.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Helen is not directly declaring her love. She uses the hypothetical proposition argumentatively to challenge Arthur’s claim that he loves her: conduct like his would make protestations of love untrustworthy. The surrounding dispute concerns fidelity, marriage vows, and the relation between professed feeling and observable behaviour, but the highlighted clause itself remains a counterfactual love-state proposition rather than a commitment or expressive outburst.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“would you think I loved you, if I did so?”\",\"supports\":\"The love proposition is embedded in a counterfactual question rather than asserted as Helen’s actual present or past state.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Just imagine yourself in my place”\",\"supports\":\"Explicitly marks the role reversal and imagined status of the highlighted event.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Would you believe my protestations, and honour and trust me under such circumstances?”\",\"supports\":\"Shows that Helen is testing the credibility of professed love against conduct, not making a new undertaking or reflexive emotional discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Arthur has just said, “You cannot hate me as long as I love you,” after Helen confronts him about his conduct toward Lady Lowborough.\",\"supports\":\"Establishes that Helen’s hypothetical answers and challenges Arthur’s preceding avowal of love.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately handles the embedded love-state content: moderate T records its representational character, while the hypothetical status and contextual interpretation capture its nonassertoric argumentative use. No residual core force requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The past tense “loved” is governed by the hypothetical construction and does not independently report that Helen’s love has ended.\"}"
        }
      ],
